)", "operator": ">", "right": "2*r"}, {"left": "Distance(P1, P4)", "operator": ">", "right": "2*r"}, {"left": "Distance(P1, P5)", "operator": ">", "right": "2*r"}, {"left": "Distance(P2, P4)", "operator": ">", "right": "2*r"}, {"left": "Distance(P2, P5)", "operator": ">", "right": "2*r"}, {"left": "Distance(P3, P5)", "operator": ">", "right": "2*r"}], "target": "Area(Outer) - Area(C0) - Area(C1) - Area(C2) - Area(C3) - Area(C4) - Area(C5)"}, "problem_statement": "Six congruent circles form a ring with each circle externally tangent to two circles adjacent to it. All circles are internally tangent to a circle $C$ with radius 30. Let $K$ be the area of the region inside circle $C$ and outside of the six circles in the ring. Find $\\lfloor K \\rfloor.$", "source": "https://web.archive.org/web/20250819010808/https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/index.php/2005_AIME_I_Problems#Problem_1", "answer": ["300*pi"], "authoring_status": "runnable", "truth_status": "verified", "truth_basis": "geometric reference verified by hand construction and converted to primary answer key", "source_evidence": {"problem_file": "source-evidence/2005_AIME_I_Problems.html", "problem_sha256": "e570f140ff39ec85d1cab4c6e18bc05596b7fb081b6c5a5cb6f188a7e762353a", "problem_section_line": 494, "answer_file": "source-evidence/2005_AIME_I_Answer_Key.html", "answer_sha256": "0cc6beb22a48465f72d64b10d431eff7804d18cd70fd5d1e57d130d78dd785be"}}
