{"id": "AIME-2012-I-14", "dedupe_key": "AIME-2012-I-14", "dsl_path": "dsl/AIME-2012-I-14.json", "dsl": {"id": "AIME-2012-I-14", "variables": ["x", "y", "u", "v"], "constructions": [{"name": "A", "expression": "Point(x, y)"}, {"name": "B", "expression": "Point(u, y)"}, {"name": "C", "expression": "Point(x, v)"}], "conditions": [{"left": "2*x+u", "operator": "=", "right": 0}, {"left": "2*y+v", "operator": "=", "right": 0}, {"left": "x**2+y**2+u**2+y**2+x**2+v**2", "operator": "=", "right": 250}, {"left": "u", "operator": ">", "right": "x"}, {"left": "v", "operator": ">", "right": "y"}], "target": "Distance(B, C)**2"}, "problem_statement": "Complex numbers $a,$ $b,$ and $c$ are zeros of a polynomial $P(z) = z^3 + qz + r,$ and $|a|^2 + |b|^2 + |c|^2 = 250.$ The points corresponding to $a,$ $b,$ and $c$ in the complex plane are the vertices of a right triangle with hypotenuse $h.$ Find $h^2.$", "source": "https://web.archive.org/web/20250819010921/https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/index.php/2012_AIME_I_Problems#Problem_14", "answer": ["375"], "authoring_status": "runnable", "truth_status": "verified", "truth_basis": "geometric reference verified by hand construction and converted to primary answer key", "source_evidence": {"problem_file": "source-evidence/2012_AIME_I_Problems.html", "problem_sha256": "e26f2633cb8354a6baa92ad70e9439b99d8831a85be5b7afb6cf7b9cd4aca5f9", "problem_section_line": 552, "answer_file": "source-evidence/2012_AIME_I_Answer_Key.html", "answer_sha256": "6bb18be5b1334e7407242c7180a6972c15aed2e3adef90dcd017c4e62fd50d8f"}}
{"id": "AIME-2014-I-01", "dedupe_key": "AIME-2014-I-01", "dsl_path": "dsl/AIME-2014-I-01.json", "dsl": {"id": "AIME-2014-I-01", "variables": [], "constructions": [{"name": "L0", "expression": "Point(0, 0*80/3)"}, {"name": "R0", "expression": "Point(50, 0*80/3)"}, {"name": "L1", "expression": "Point(0, 1*80/3)"}, {"name": "R1", "expression": "Point(50, 1*80/3)"}, {"name": "L2", "expression": "Point(0, 2*80/3)"}, {"name": "R2", "expression": "Point(50, 2*80/3)"}, {"name": "L3", "expression": "Point(0, 3*80/3)"}, {"name": "R3", "expression": "Point(50, 3*80/3)"}], "conditions": [], "target": "Distance(L0, R0) + Distance(L0, R1) + Distance(R0, L1) + Distance(L1, R2) + Distance(R1, L2) + Distance(L2, R3) + Distance(R2, L3) + 2*200"}, "problem_statement": "The 8 eyelets for the lace of a sneaker all lie on a rectangle, four equally spaced on each of the longer sides. The rectangle has a width of 50 mm and a length of 80 mm. There is one eyelet at each vertex of the rectangle. The lace itself must pass between the vertex eyelets along a width side of the rectangle and then crisscross between successive eyelets until it reaches the two eyelets at the other width side of the rectangle as shown. After passing through these final eyelets, each of the ends of the lace must extend at least 200 mm farther to allow a knot to be tied. Find the minimum length of the lace in millimeters. \n\n\n[asy] size(200); defaultpen(linewidth(0.7)); path laceL=(-20,-30)..tension 0.75 ..(-90,-135)..(-102,-147)..(-152,-150)..tension 2 ..(-155,-140)..(-135,-40)..(-50,-4)..tension 0.8 ..origin; path laceR=reflect((75,0),(75,-240))*laceL; draw(origin--(0,-240)--(150,-240)--(150,0)--cycle,gray); for(int i=0;i<=3;i=i+1) { path circ1=circle((0,-80*i),5),circ2=circle((150,-80*i),5); unfill(circ1); draw(circ1); unfill(circ2); draw(circ2); } draw(laceL--(150,-80)--(0,-160)--(150,-240)--(0,-240)--(150,-160)--(0,-80)--(150,0)^^laceR,linewidth(1));[/asy]\nSolution", "source": "https://web.archive.org/web/20240713184355/https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/index.php/2014_AIME_I_Problems#Problem_1", "answer": ["790"], "authoring_status": "runnable", "truth_status": "verified", "truth_basis": "geometric reference verified by hand construction and converted to primary answer key", "source_evidence": {"problem_file": "source-evidence/2014_AIME_I_Problems.html", "problem_sha256": "43911d45267f06ed7327ceaf50d3737af40204fb00d4232f77d47174baa18196", "problem_section_line": 498, "answer_file": "source-evidence/2014_AIME_I_Answer_Key.html", "answer_sha256": "96bd14a6bf7e04f2e083cc2eb0d827500d0a4483807280f7ca87e4e69a84e510"}}
{"id": "AIME-2014-I-10", "dedupe_key": "AIME-2014-I-10", "dsl_path": "dsl/AIME-2014-I-10.json", "dsl": {"id": "AIME-2014-I-10", "variables": ["theta"], "constructions": [{"name": "E", "expression": "Point(0, 0)"}, {"name": "A", "expression": "Point(5, 0)"}, {"name": "C", "expression": "Point(5+1, 0)"}, {"name": "D", "expression": "Point((5+1)*cos(theta), (5+1)*sin(theta))"}, {"name": "B", "expression": "Point(D.x-1, D.y)"}], "conditions": [{"left": "(5+1)*theta", "operator": "=", "right": "2*pi*1"}, {"left": "theta", "operator": ">", "right": 0}, {"left": "theta", "operator": "<", "right": "2*pi"}], "target": "sin(Angle(B, E, A))**2"}, "problem_statement": "A disk with radius $1$ is externally tangent to a disk with radius $5$. Let $A$ be the point where the disks are tangent, $C$ be the center of the smaller disk, and $E$ be the center of the larger disk. While the larger disk remains fixed, the smaller disk is allowed to roll along the outside of the larger disk until the smaller disk has turned through an angle of $360^\\circ$. That is, if the center of the smaller disk has moved to the point $D$, and the point on the smaller disk that began at $A$ has now moved to point $B$, then $\\overline{AC}$ is parallel to $\\overline{BD}$. Then $\\sin^2(\\angle BEA)=\\tfrac{m}{n}$, where $m$ and $n$ are relatively prime positive integers. Find $m+n$.", "source": "https://web.archive.org/web/20240713184355/https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/index.php/2014_AIME_I_Problems#Problem_10", "answer": ["27/31"], "authoring_status": "runnable", "truth_status": "verified", "truth_basis": "geometric reference verified by hand construction and converted to primary answer key", "source_evidence": {"problem_file": "source-evidence/2014_AIME_I_Problems.html", "problem_sha256": "43911d45267f06ed7327ceaf50d3737af40204fb00d4232f77d47174baa18196", "problem_section_line": 535, "answer_file": "source-evidence/2014_AIME_I_Answer_Key.html", "answer_sha256": "96bd14a6bf7e04f2e083cc2eb0d827500d0a4483807280f7ca87e4e69a84e510"}}
{"id": "AIME-2014-II-10", "dedupe_key": "AIME-2014-II-10", "dsl_path": "dsl/AIME-2014-II-10.json", "dsl": {"id": "AIME-2014-II-10", "variables": ["x", "y", "u", "v"], "constructions": [{"name": "Z", "expression": "Point(2014, 0)"}, {"name": "W1", "expression": "Point(x, y)"}, {"name": "W2", "expression": "Point(u, v)"}], "conditions": [{"left": "x**2-y**2+2014*x+2014**2", "operator": "=", "right": 0}, {"left": "2*x*y+2014*y", "operator": "=", "right": 0}, {"left": "x**2+y**2", "operator": ">", "right": 0}, {"left": "(x+2014)**2+y**2", "operator": ">", "right": 0}, {"left": "u**2-v**2+2014*u+2014**2", "operator": "=", "right": 0}, {"left": "2*u*v+2014*v", "operator": "=", "right": 0}, {"left": "u**2+v**2", "operator": ">", "right": 0}, {"left": "(u+2014)**2+v**2", "operator": ">", "right": 0}, {"left": "y", "operator": ">", "right": 0}, {"left": "v", "operator": "<", "right": 0}], "target": "Area(Z, W1, W2)"}, "problem_statement": "Let $z$ be a complex number with $|z|=2014$. Let $P$ be the polygon in the complex plane whose vertices are $z$ and every $w$ such that $\\frac{1}{z+w}=\\frac{1}{z}+\\frac{1}{w}$. Then the area enclosed by $P$ can be written in the form $n\\sqrt{3}$, where $n$ is an integer. Find the remainder when $n$ is divided by $1000$. \n\n\n\n\nSolution", "source": "https://web.archive.org/web/20250819010455/https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/index.php/2014_AIME_II_Problems#Problem_10", "answer": ["3042147*sqrt(3)"], "authoring_status": "runnable", "truth_status": "verified", "truth_basis": "geometric reference verified by hand construction and converted to primary answer key", "source_evidence": {"problem_file": "source-evidence/2014_AIME_II_Problems.html", "problem_sha256": "6371fcc5ba834afeb7be140a422b745c0d0228128b5b9a08e49c18ceb220c845", "problem_section_line": 538, "answer_file": "source-evidence/2014_AIME_II_Answer_Key.html", "answer_sha256": "2d3a8bf30b8d12d2035fae37e070339c211c746c261d80914d2e06e69c31b6ad"}}
{"id": "AIME-2019-I-12", "dedupe_key": "AIME-2019-I-12", "dsl_path": "dsl/AIME-2019-I-12.json", "dsl": {"id": "AIME-2019-I-12", "variables": ["x"], "constructions": [{"name": "Z", "expression": "Point(x, 11)"}, {"name": "F", "expression": "Point(x**2-11**2-19*x, 2*11*x-19*11)"}, {"name": "G", "expression": "Point(F.x**2-F.y**2-19*F.x, 2*F.x*F.y-19*F.y)"}], "conditions": [{"left": "Dot(Vector(Z, F), Vector(F, G))", "operator": "=", "right": 0}, {"left": "Distance(Z, F)", "operator": ">", "right": 0}, {"left": "Distance(F, G)", "operator": ">", "right": 0}, {"left": "x", "operator": ">", "right": 0}], "target": "Z.x"}, "problem_statement": "Given $f(z) = z^2-19z$, there are complex numbers $z$ with the property that $z$, $f(z)$, and $f(f(z))$ are the vertices of a right triangle in the complex plane with a right angle at $f(z)$. There are positive integers $m$ and $n$ such that one such value of $z$ is $m+\\sqrt{n}+11i$. Find $m+n$.", "source": "https://web.archive.org/web/20250819011031/https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/index.php/2019_AIME_I_Problems#Problem_12", "answer": ["9+sqrt(221)"], "authoring_status": "runnable", "truth_status": "verified", "truth_basis": "geometric reference verified by hand construction and converted to primary answer key", "source_evidence": {"problem_file": "source-evidence/2019_AIME_I_Problems.html", "problem_sha256": "b8766e9413d0a05e82c09a63606f2ac50b732d14e2c66b38fff02311bc93865f", "problem_section_line": 543, "answer_file": "source-evidence/2019_AIME_I_Answer_Key.html", "answer_sha256": "e367371c6d1801761f0729382ae0df87d0b37842fe675ac6dae2210416ca621f"}}
